+<section class="article" data-lang="en">
+<h2>What a seal has in common with radioactive decay — and what it does not</h2>
+<p>The 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics awarded to Francis Halzen recognizes decisive contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory and the discovery of high-energy neutrinos of astrophysical origin. IceCube does not “see” a cosmic neutrino the way a camera sees an object. It reconstructs a rare event from light traces produced when an interaction occurs in Antarctic ice. That gives us a useful starting point: physics often works not with the past event itself, but with a trace whose production rule can be tested.</p>
+<p>Radioactive decay makes the same temporal structure especially clear. A single unstable nucleus decays spontaneously, and its exact decay time cannot be predicted. For a large ensemble, the probability of transition per unit time is characteristic, and the number of undecayed nuclei follows an exponential law. A half-life therefore does not tell us when <em>this particular</em> nucleus will decay. It describes how a population changes through time.</p>
+<p class="formula">N(t) = N₀ · e<sup>−λt</sup> &nbsp; · &nbsp; T<sub>1/2</sub> = ln(2)/λ</p>
+<p>The QIK‑VRT seal is not radioactive physics. Its 25-year interval is neither a half-life nor a decay probability. It is a deliberately defined continuity rule: before the interval expires, a person or institution must create a successor generation that binds all predecessor digests and adds a cryptographic method considered strong at that time. If this does not happen, the history is not erased, but its status drops from <em>admissible</em> to <em>historical</em>.</p>
+<p>The structural commonality is therefore deeper: <strong>state → time → transition → trace → reconstruction.</strong> In radioactive decay, nature produces the transition and the measurable trace. In resealing, a responsible actor produces the transition and the verifiable record. In both cases, pointing to an “old object” is not enough. What matters is whether the chain of state changes lets a later observer reconstruct what happened.</p>
+<p>Put more sharply: radioactive decay teaches that identity through time does not mean that nothing happens. A population of nuclei changes precisely <em>through</em> transitions. Long-term evidence works similarly in form, but normatively rather than physically: it remains connectable when each transition preserves provenance. A new seal must not erase the old one; it must carry it forward.</p>
+<p class="quote">Long-term reliability does not mean “everything stays the same.” It means “every change leaves its origin verifiable.”</p>
+<div class="boundary"><strong>Boundary of the analogy.</strong> Radioactive decay is spontaneous, stochastic and physical; QIK‑VRT resealing is intentional, institutional and cryptographic. This does not claim that information is radioactive, that a digital artifact will survive for 584 years, or that any signal travels backward in time. Nor does it prove or model phenomenal subjective experience. The analogy concerns only the form of time-bound transitions and their traces.</div>
+<h3>Short version</h3>
+<p class="short">Radioactive decay and QIK‑VRT resealing share a temporal form: a state becomes intelligible not by being kept perfectly unchanged, but through a traceable sequence of transitions. In decay, nature supplies the law; in QIK‑VRT, people and institutions supply the continuity rule. One happens spontaneously, the other only if someone takes responsibility. The common lesson is not “information is radioactive,” but: <strong>time must be part of the verification rule.</strong></p>
+</section>
